    "title": "What's open and closed on Easter Sunday in Connecticut",
    "text": "A woman shops for eggs in a grocery store.Vladdeep/iStock via Getty ImagesEaster is this Sunday, following Holy Week, which consists of sacred days for Christians across the world, including Palm Sunday and Good Friday.Unlike Good Friday, Easter Sunday is not a state holiday in Connecticut. It's also not a federal one. Regardless, many businesses choose to close or operate under limited hours so that employees can spend the holiday with their families.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Some restaurants will remain open to offer Easter brunches and dinners. Other businesses, such as grocery stores and clothing retailers, might be closed, so it's best to check with specific stores before going.Operations like the stock market, government offices and Connecticut Lottery are always closed on weekends. Most banks, DMV and post offices are closed on Sundays.Here are some of the establishments in Connecticut that are affected by Easter Sunday: Which stores are closed on Easter Sunday in Connecticut?Here are some of the stores that have announced closures on Easter Sunday:Article continues below this adMake CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceGreenwich Historical Society holds its annual Easter Egg Hunt in Greenwich, Conn., on Friday April 7, 2023. In addition to three hunts during the day, there was also a musical instrument \"petting zoo,\" a bubble show & DJ music by Magical Memories, snap a selfie with the Easter Bunny, Chicken Joe's Food Truck, cookie decorating, Easter crafts, games and delicious treats.Christian Abraham/Hearst Connecticut MediaWhich grocery stores will be open on Easter Sunday in Connecticut?Many of the most popular grocery stores in Connecticut will remain open for normal operating hours on Easter Sunday, while some have limited operating hours. Shoppers should check with their local grocery store location to confirm hours of operation ahead of time.Article continues below this adHere are some of the operating hours of local and chain grocery stores across the Nutmeg State on Easter Sunday: ACME Markets: 7 a.m. to 9 p.m. Balducci's: 7 a.m. to 10 p.m. Caraluzzi's: 7 a.m. to 6 p.m. Food Bazaar Supermarket: 7 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. Price Chopper: 6 a.m. to 3 p.m. Price Rite: 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. ShopRite: various locations will be closed, while others will have limited operating hours. Stew Leonard's: 7 a.m. to 6 p.m. (Newington); 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. (Danbury and Norwalk) Stop & Shop: 7 a.m. to 5 p.m. The Fresh Market: 8 a.m. to 9 p.m. Trader Joe's: 8 a.m. to 9 p.m. Whole Foods Market: hours vary by location, but most stores will be open from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. What museums and aquariums will be closed on Easter Sunday in Connecticut?Many museums and aquariums in Connecticut are open on Easter Sunday, including Mystic Aquarium, which is one of the places in Connecticut to see the Easter Bunny. Here are some that will be closed for the holiday:Article continues below this adGet a daily news briefing and subscriber-exclusive reporting.",
